course: technology
lesson: 19
title: Electric cars and charging
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: An electric car has no engine burning fuel: a big battery under the floor powers electric motors that turn the wheels, so there are no explosions, no exhaust and hardly any noise.
explain: Instead of going to a petrol pump, it is plugged into a charging point, at home overnight or at a fast charger on a long trip.
explain: It even makes some electricity back when it slows down: the motor works backwards like a bike dynamo and puts energy back into the battery.
explain: Picture standing at the kerb as one glides past with only a soft hum: that quietness is lovely for the street, and it is exactly why you look as well as listen before crossing.
explain: Batteries hold a lot of energy, so the rules are simple: don't play with charging cables, and tell an adult about any battery that is damaged, hot or smells.
fact: An electric car has a big battery under the floor that powers electric motors to turn the wheels, with no engine burning fuel.
fact: Electric cars are very quiet, so new ones must make a gentle sound at low speed so people can hear them coming.
fact: A charging point sends electricity from the grid (the country's network of power lines) along a cable into the car's battery; a fast charger can add a lot of range in about twenty to thirty minutes, while home charging usually takes hours.
fact: Charging cables carry high power, so children never play with them or their plugs.
fact: In regenerative braking the motor works backwards as a generator, a machine that makes electricity, when the car slows, putting some energy back into the battery, and some electric cars can almost stop without the brake pedal.
fact: A hybrid car has both a petrol engine and an electric motor with a small battery, and it switches between them or uses both, often driving silently at low speed in towns and starting the petrol engine when more power is needed, as on a hill.
fact: An electric car's battery pack is thousands of small lithium cells joined together, with cooling to keep them at the right temperature.
fact: Damaged car batteries can catch fire and fire crews have special training for them; old car batteries can be reused to store electricity for buildings.
fact: In the UK it is illegal to ride a private e-scooter on public roads and pavements; only rental scooters in trial areas are allowed, and only for older riders with a driving licence.
fact: E-scooter and e-bike battery fires have become a big worry for fire services.
fact: Electric buses charge at the depot overnight or quickly at some stops; London was reported to have more than a thousand of them.
fact: A hydrogen vehicle mixes hydrogen with oxygen from the air in a fuel cell to make electricity, and the only thing from its exhaust is water; hydrogen is still hard to make cleanly and has few filling stations.
fact: The range is how far an electric car can go on one charge; it depends on battery size, speed, weather and heating, and cold weather shortens it, because batteries work less well in the cold and heating the car uses power.
fact: With no engine in the front, some electric cars have a small boot there, called a frunk, meaning front trunk.
exercise: What turns the wheels of an electric car? | Electric motors powered by a big battery | A small petrol engine hidden under the seats | A spring wound up at night | Wind blowing into the front
exercise: Why do new electric cars make a humming sound when they go slowly? | So people can hear them coming | Because the battery is too full | To scare away birds | Because the motor is broken
exercise: What does a charging point do? | Sends electricity from the grid into the car's battery | Fills the car with petrol | Pumps air into the tyres | Washes the car while it waits
exercise: About how long can a fast charger take to add a lot of range? | About twenty to thirty minutes | Only about two seconds, like a pump | About a whole week, day and night | About a month, so you charge in spring
exercise: How can braking make electricity in an electric car? | The motor works backwards as a generator | The brake pads rub out sparks into the battery | The wheels catch lightning | The driver's foot pushes electricity in
exercise: What is a hybrid car? | A car with both a petrol engine and an electric motor | A car that can fly and drive | A car with two steering wheels | A car that runs on water only
exercise: Why does a hybrid sometimes make no noise at all? | It drives on its electric motor at low speed | Its engine is wrapped in a thick blanket to keep it quiet | It is rolling downhill only | Its radio covers the noise
exercise: What is an electric car's battery pack made of? | Thousands of small lithium cells joined together | One giant ordinary torch battery, the size of a sofa | A tank of liquid electricity | Lots of car engines glued together
exercise: What can happen to an electric car battery when it gets old? | It can be reused to store electricity for buildings | It turns into petrol | It must be buried deep at the bottom of the sea so it cannot hurt anyone | It grows back to new by itself
exercise: Can children in the UK ride their own e-scooter on public roads? | No, private e-scooters are illegal on public roads and pavements | Yes, anywhere they like | Yes, but only on pavements | Yes, if they wear a helmet
exercise: Why do fire services worry about e-scooters and e-bikes? | Their batteries can catch fire | They are too fast for fire engines | They block fire hydrants | They make too much noise
exercise: When do electric buses usually charge? | At the depot overnight, or quickly at some stops | Only while driving along in the rain, from the wet road | Never, they run on diesel | From passengers' phones
exercise: What is the only thing from a hydrogen vehicle's exhaust? | Water | Black smoke | Carbon monoxide | Petrol fumes
exercise: If hydrogen only makes water, why isn't every car using it? | It is hard to make cleanly and has few filling stations | Water is bad for roads | Hydrogen cars can only go backwards | It is not allowed in any country
exercise: What is the range of an electric car? | How far it can go on one charge | How fast it can go on a motorway | How many seats it has for passengers | How long its charging cable can stretch
exercise: Why do electric cars go less far in winter? | Batteries work less well in the cold and heating uses power | Snow makes the car heavier and heavier | Cold air blocks the charging point | Winter roads are longer
exercise: What is a frunk? | A small boot at the front of some electric cars | A spare tyre kept up on the roof | The battery under the floor of the car, where the engine would sit | A horn that plays a tune
exercise: The charger adds 3 miles every minute. How many miles after 10 minutes? | 30 | 13 | 3 | 300
test: my dad says electric cars are silent so no need to look, just listen. Right? | No, look as well as listen; they can be very quiet | Yes, you'll always hear them a long way off, they roar | Yes, they always beep at you | No, but only at night
test: What would happen to an electric car's range on a freezing day with the heater on full? | It would go less far on one charge | It would go much further | Nothing would change | It would charge itself from the cold
test: oi, can I swing on the charging cable while the car charges? | No, never play with charging cables or plugs | Yes, it's just a rope | Yes, if you hold the plug end tight and don't swing too high | No, but you can pull the plug out
test: Mia's friend got a private e-scooter for her birthday and rides it on the street. Is that allowed in the UK? | No, private e-scooters are illegal on public roads | Yes, it is legal for under-tens, as long as a grown-up is watching | Yes, if she rides fast | No, only on motorways
test: A car slows down without the driver touching the brake pedal, and the battery number goes up. What happened? | Regenerative braking turned movement back into electricity | The battery magically refilled | The car found a hidden charger | The driver turned the radio off
test: Which car makes nothing but water from its exhaust? | A hydrogen fuel cell car | A brand-new diesel car | A small petrol car | An old steam train
test: Jake looks under the bonnet of an electric car and finds space for a bag. Why? | There is no engine there, so some have a frunk | The engine is hidden in the bag | The battery has fallen out | It is where the petrol goes in for the small engine that starts the car
test: Ruby reckons an electric car stops at the petrol station. Right? | No, it charges at a charging point | Yes, it needs a little petrol to start | Yes, it fills its tyres with fuel | No, it never needs any energy
test: An e-bike battery in the hall is hot and smells strange. What do you do? | Keep away and tell an adult at once | Put it in the bath to cool it down quickly | Wrap it up tight in a thick wool blanket | Charge it again to fix it, then check it
test: In a cartoon an electric car's battery lasts for ever without charging. What's true? | It must be charged; its range runs out | Batteries never empty | It charges from sunshine through the roof only | It runs on air once the battery is flat
test: A hybrid glides past the school silently, then the engine starts on the hill. Why? | It used the electric motor, then the petrol engine for more power | The engine was asleep | The hill switched it on | The driver honked by mistake
exam: Identify the principle by which an electric vehicle recovers energy during deceleration. | The motor operates as a generator and recharges the battery | The brake pads convert friction into stored fuel | The tyres collect static electricity from the road | The vehicle draws energy from overhead cables
exam: It is given that an electric vehicle is driven in freezing conditions with cabin heating in use. Ascertain the effect upon its range. | The range is reduced | The range is increased | The range is unaffected | The range becomes unlimited
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the legal position of privately owned electric scooters in the United Kingdom? | Their use upon public roads and pavements is unlawful | Their use is lawful for all persons on all roads | Their use is lawful upon pavements only | Their use is lawful for children under twelve when accompanied by a parent
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the emissions of a hydrogen fuel cell vehicle. | Water is the sole product emitted | Carbon monoxide is emitted in quantity | Black smoke is emitted continuously | Petrol vapour is emitted whilst at idle
